Z||000000||strdream||12-09-2000||01:55 PM||||OK!  It's time for an update..<P>First off, Thanks for all of the replies.  It realey helps in makeing a decision.<BR>  <BR>I called to order the '88 suspesion parts from the salvage yard.  They couldn't remember the preveous inquiery (wouldn't you know) so I swallowed hard and ordered the whole front suspension, crossmember and all. they said it will be here next week.  <P>Also I got my pattern back from the foundry to make the upgrades for the bigger bearings and brakes.<P>I talked with Tim at the Fiero shop in Richmond Cal.  He asked If I could make spindles with less drop.  1" 1 1/4"  1 1/2".<BR>I am leaning twards the 1 1/4" drop.  I can do it easily enough using the stock brakes and apply the bigger upgrade as it becomes availible.  <P>Any input?<P>Still wiating for more correspondence from Pontiaddict<P>Ross<P>P.S.  19 more pair of casting just came in<p>[This message has been edited by strdream (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||12-09-2000||03:50 PM||||Strdream, I didn't know you were looking for me. I just looked at your other post and unfortunately, I don't know as much as you think. I know the 4th gen F-body uses bolt in hubs after looking at brake swaps and stumbling onto it. Since you were designing the spindles and can make modifications to the casting, I figured that you might be able to upgrade the design to accomidate the different hubs. I used to have a 4x4 S-10 so I know they had CV front axles and bolt on hubs. I don't know if the spline count or diameter are the same as a Fiero but it might be possible to change the outer part of the cv joint to the S-10 part to make it work. It all seems possible but I don't have all of the technical info to be sure. <BR>||||reg||1
